Step 5: Visualise missingness to see how sparse the data is

```{r visualise-missingness, warning=FALSE, out.width="90%"}

df <- read.csv("M:/df_atleast1.csv")

# Count missingness
df_missingness <- df[,-1]

# Count missingness
missingness <- colSums(is.na(df_missingness))

# Summary table
summary(missingness)

# Histogram
hist_missingness <- hist(missingness, ylim = c(0,4200))
text(x = hist_missingness$mids, y = hist_missingness$counts, labels = hist_missingness$counts, pos = 3, col = "black", cex = 0.8)

# Boxplot
box_missingness <- boxplot(missingness, main = "Boxplot of missingness", horizontal = TRUE)
quartiles <- as.integer(round(box_missingness$stats[,1],2))
text(x = quartiles, y = c(1.05, 1.25, 1.25), labels = quartiles, pos = 3, col = "black", cex = 0.5)

```
<p>
<h5>Observations:</h5>

* Minimum no. of missing observations is 43264; i.e., max no. of observations an item has is 45965-43264 = 2701 responses
    
* Median no. of missing observations is 45798; i.e., median no. of observations an item has is 45965-45798 = 167 responses

* This means filtering out data with less than 167 responses will cut the number of items in half
</p>

