Step 6: Further data cleaning
<p>
* Remove participants with less than 5 responses
</p>
```{r only-one-response, eval=FALSE}

# Load data
df <- read.csv("M:/df_atleast1.csv")

# Count no. of rows with 5 or less responses
counts <- sapply(1:5, function(i) sum(rowSums(!is.na(df)) == i))
names(counts) <- paste0("only", 1:5)

# Show table
counts

# Remove rows with less than 5 responses
df <- df[rowSums(!is.na(df)) > 5, ]

# Now all items have at least 6 responses
write.csv(df, "atleast6_df.csv", row.names = FALSE)

```
<br>
<p>
Now there are two options to choose from at this point: 
1. Use bigIRT (built for very sparse dataframes) to extract word difficulty measures through Rasch modelling
2. Further pre-processing by removing words with less than a certain threshold of observations (e.g., filter out items with < 167 responses)

* A possibility is to run (1) first, and if it fails, then do (2) and then (1) again
* Note that all the items have at least 1 response at this point
</p>




